WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.380
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Dr. Bil'in Jalal, who's a researcher

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:10.000
at Harvard University and visiting researcher
at Cambridge University, one of the world's

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.460
leading experts, on sleep paralysis.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:14.639
It's really great to have you on.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:15.639
I appreciate it.

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:17.840
Speaker 4: Oh, I'm glad to be here, David.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:24.880
Speaker 1: So sleep, sleep is such an interesting
field because it seems to me it's difficult

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.570
to study it without affecting one's sleep
in the sense that many of the settings in

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:36.440
which sleep is studied, like sleep labs, for
example, are very unnatural situations to

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:41.110
do something that most of us do every day,
which is sleep asleep while being observed,

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.809
sleep with electrodes on etc.

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:48.690
It seems that it's difficult to get great
data for that reason.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.090
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:51.090
Is that true?

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.090
Speaker 4: Yeah.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.090
Yeah, that's true.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:57.350
I mean, you know, bringing somebody into a
sleep lab and hooking them up with these electrodes

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:02.430
and things like that and having them sleep
in a sleep lab is obviously not, as, you know,

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.749
useful as as the home environment where you
naturally sleep and things like that.

00:01:06.749 --> 00:01:12.189
So I agree it is difficult, but nonetheless,
it's it's doable.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:14.250
But I agree, you know, we don't have that.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.329
So it's a bit of a tricky thing in that way.

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:22.079
Speaker 1: Let's maybe start and talk a little
bit about sleep paralysis now.

00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:28.420
To some degree, in certain phases of sleep,
we are paralyzed in the sense that we might

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.539
be dreaming of taking some physical action
and you aren't taking that physical action

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:35.609
in the real world.

00:01:35.609 --> 00:01:39.789
You might be punching someone in a dream,
but your body is not going out and punching

00:01:39.789 --> 00:01:41.009
into the air.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:44.319
And that's a good thing when it happens in
the in the correct times.

00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:45.469
Is that right?

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:47.390
Speaker 4: That's right, David.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:52.340
So each night we cycle through these 90 90
minute cycles of sleep.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:57.729
And if you sleep eight hours, I hope you do,
then you have like maybe four of these blocks.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:01.829
And during one of these stages, you go through
something called REM sleep, and during REM

00:02:01.829 --> 00:02:04.710
sleep, you have these bizarre and lifelike
crisp dreams.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:07.619
You know, the ones where you see yourself
on the moon, wrestling with an alligator and

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:10.519
then having tea with the Queen, everything
is just messed up, you know?

00:02:10.519 --> 00:02:13.440
So during these dreams, you certainly don't
want to act these dreams out and hurt your

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.530
sleeping partner.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:19.879
So your brain essentially the lower part of
the brain, paralyzes your entire body.

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:22.700
And so, yeah, you have this paralysis each
night.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:26.170
You know that that you you go through pretty
much now

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.920
Speaker 1: when we talk about sleep paralysis
as a as a phenomena and condition, what we're

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:36.859
really talking about is outside of REM sleep,
the body remaining paralyzed

00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:40.849
Speaker 4: while sleep paralysis is basically
this like.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:45.620
So you are in this paralysis state during
this REM stage of sleep and then suddenly

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:51.079
parts of the brain that has to do with sort
of sort of wakefulness centers of the brain

00:02:51.079 --> 00:02:55.280
cortical regions, they become prematurely
activated.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.349
So you start waking up in a way even though
you're still paralyzed in REM.

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:04.140
So it's at this synchrony to the architecture
of REM, whereby your brain suddenly switches

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.400
so it's awake and sleeping at the same, you
know, in the same way at the same time.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.209
So you have this clash of wakefulness and
REM paralysis occurring at the same time.

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:17.409
So that what what sleep paralysis
Speaker 1: is, how common is that?

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:21.930
Speaker 4: You know, about 20 percent of the
population have experienced sleep paralysis

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.290
at least one time.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.209
But then you have maybe one or two percent
that have it more chronically.

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:32.180
So they will have sleep paralysis, you know,
two or three times a day or a week or, you

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:34.280
know, five or six times a month.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:41.540
So it is really but at least one in at least
20 percent have it have had it in one at least

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:42.540
once in their life?

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:47.989
Speaker 1: Is it is it a bad thing in the
sense of is it a condition that we that we

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:50.870
are we try to treat, I guess, is the question.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:55.819
Speaker 4: Right, so if you have it just like
a regular person, you have it once or twice

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:58.370
in your life, and indeed it can be scary.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:01.970
I mean, you wake up paralyzed, you think you
might think you're going to die, perhaps.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:05.340
And then there's a ghost in your room because
typically you have you hallucinate during

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.870
these experiences.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:11.959
And so that is terrifying and frightening,
but it's not pathological and so on in its

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:12.959
own right, right?

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:15.040
It's just, you know, scary and things like
that.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.680
But if you have it chronically, it becomes
a Paris Omnia, you know, so it's a fearful

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.552
thing that keeps occurring in your life.

00:04:22.552 --> 00:04:26.449
And our research suggests if you give this
too much meaning and you say this is a demon

00:04:26.449 --> 00:04:30.410
chasing me, I'm being possessed and things
like that which can occur in certain cultures,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:34.940
well, then potentially you could even lead
to things like anxiety and maybe even trauma

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.730
from just, you know, being overwhelmed by
these, this paralysis and having these, you

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:41.000
know, scary hallucinations.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:46.530
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit about the
dream component of sleep.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:52.970
There is so much literature going back so
long with different ideas about dream meaning

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.570
and dream interpretation.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.580
There are one side is it's completely random.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.830
There's no connection whatsoever between dreams
and what's happening in the real world for

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:05.830
you.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:10.680
There is another side which is know that the
dreams are a direct response to things that

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:14.930
are taking place in someone's life, and there
can be sort of specific meaning assigned to

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:15.930
them.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:20.720
Let's start with maybe some of the common
dream tropes that a lot of people report.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:27.250
So like falling in a dream and waking up before
you hit the ground is a very common theme.

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.680
Teeth falling out can be another common one.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.470
The idea that you're kind of running or walking,
but it's almost like you're in molasses, like

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:37.539
it's very, very difficult to move.

00:05:37.539 --> 00:05:41.410
You know, there's these common themes that
a lot of people seem to experience.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:46.050
So it seems like it would follow that there
must be some meaning to those, at least.

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.300
Speaker 4: Absolutely.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:48.300
There is.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.300
There's a pattern, right?

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.300
So let's take them one by one.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.889
So you mentioned the tooth falling out.

00:05:53.889 --> 00:05:57.020
So that seems to be due to sort of the end.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.280
So all of us, right?

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:03.230
And at a certain age, when are children have
teeth literally falling out of our mouth?

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:05.729
And imagine this must be very anxiety provoking.

00:06:05.729 --> 00:06:10.140
So obviously you will lay down memories of
this fear and anxiety that will remain with

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.860
you for many people, the rest of their lives.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:16.500
But they can really, you know, pin down like,
why do I have this dream?

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.330
Why am I terrified of my dreams falling out?

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:23.129
Well, there was a period in our life where
our teeth were loose and we're literally falling

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:24.129
out.

00:06:24.129 --> 00:06:28.710
So yes, you lay down these memories and the
memory parts of your brain and then you will

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.370
dream about them off.

00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:34.650
Another very common dream is like being late
for an exam, even though, I mean, you're 40,

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:36.319
50, 60, you're running your business.

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:38.520
You're dreaming about being late for an exam,
right?

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.520
So that's a common one.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.889
Then you mentioned the thing where you're
trying to, you know, run, you know, you can't

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:43.889
get away.

00:06:43.889 --> 00:06:44.889
What's going on there?

00:06:44.889 --> 00:06:49.110
Well, it turns out you're at your dream during
REM sleep during this dream stage of sleep

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:53.580
have a has a certain physiology and a certain
neural pattern.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.699
And one of these the signatures of this is
that your the motor part of the brain, sort

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:04.039
of this part of the brain that has to do with
movement is sort of is not fueled by the chemicals

00:07:04.039 --> 00:07:06.690
it needs to move, OK?

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.860
And but the emotional part of the brain is
hyperactive.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:14.659
That's a chemical called as either choline
and the emotional parts of the brain are overactive.

00:07:14.659 --> 00:07:18.389
So you have this clash between your motor,
part of the brain, the movement, part of the

00:07:18.389 --> 00:07:22.800
brain being weakened and then the emotional
part of the brain instinctual, part of the

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.229
brain being overactive.

00:07:24.229 --> 00:07:28.300
And that manifests as this you this dream
where you are running.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.500
But you are really slow, right?

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.509
You're in quicksand and then this monster
is chasing you.

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:35.379
So that is an example of that.

00:07:35.379 --> 00:07:38.289
And what you mentioned, the other one,
Speaker 1: the falling one, just falling and

00:07:38.289 --> 00:07:40.550
waking up, you know, before
Speaker 4: you wake up.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:45.350
I think that's unclear, but probably because
we used to dream in your sleep in high places,

00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:47.659
right, like trees and things like that.

00:07:47.659 --> 00:07:52.759
So it makes sense to have a reflex of, you
know, being able to catch yourself before

00:07:52.759 --> 00:07:53.759
you fall.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:58.949
So perhaps your brain has remnants of this
in its, you know, memory centers.

00:07:58.949 --> 00:08:04.100
But you know, in short, your brain has a certain
chemistry during the dream stage, right?

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:05.790
So I ignore adrenaline.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:10.441
This chemical that allows us to focus in on
being really focused is absent during the

00:08:10.441 --> 00:08:13.860
dream stage, and that means things are like
space.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.860
You can't really focus on a specific thing,
but it's more like the world is bizarre and

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.530
time is this and places are all warped.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.400
So that's a signature of that, you know?

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.000
Or for example, there's no logic or a coherent
